        {
          "section_type": "example_question",
          "normalized_html": "<p>Express the inequality <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1 \\le x \\le4]</span> in interval notation.</p>",
          "readable_text": "Express the inequality [MATH: 1 \\le x \\le4] in interval notation.",
          "images": [],
          "free_entry_blanks": [],
          "select_lists": []
        },
        {
          "section_type": "content",
          "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
          "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
          "images": [],
          "free_entry_blanks": [],
          "select_lists": []
        },
        {
          "section_type": "example_explanation",
          "normalized_html": "<p>Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:</p><ul><li><p>Parentheses <span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}]</span> if the endpoints are <em>not</em> included. </p></li><li><p>Brackets <span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}]</span> if the endpoints <em>are</em> included.</p></li></ul><p>Our inequality contains only &quot;or equal to&quot; symbols. So, the endpoints <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 4]</span> <em>are</em> included. </p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4]</span></p><p>Therefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}]</span></p><p>More formally, we can also write</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: x\\in [1,4].]</span></p>",
          "readable_text": "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses [MATH: {\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}] if the endpoints are not included.\n- Brackets [MATH: {\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}] if the endpoints are included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints [MATH: 1] and [MATH: 4] are included.\n\n[MATH: 1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4]\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}]\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n[MATH: x\\in [1,4].]",
          "images": [],
          "free_entry_blanks": [],
          "select_lists": []
        }
      ],
      "images": [],
      "free_entry_blanks": [],
      "select_lists": [],
      "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nExpress the inequality [MATH: 1 \\le x \\le4] in interval notation.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nEvery inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses [MATH: {\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}] if the endpoints are not included.\n- Brackets [MATH: {\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}] if the endpoints are included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints [MATH: 1] and [MATH: 4] are included.\n\n[MATH: 1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4]\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}]\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n[MATH: x\\in [1,4].]"
    },
